    "result": "I implemented `RateLimiter` in `solution.py` and wrote 34 tests in `tests/test_solution.py`; `python -m pytest -q tests` now passes (34 passed). The limiter keeps a per-client list of `(time, cost)` records and drops entries once `now - t >= window_seconds`. Constructor and `cost` arguments are validated per the spec, with `bool` rejected. Time monotonicity is checked across all clients and all three methods. `retry_after` walks the live records oldest-first to find when enough units will have expired. One ambiguity I resolved myself: a call that fails validation does not advance the \"largest `now` seen\" marker, but any call that passes validation does, including rejected `allow` calls. My first test run had one failure, which was a wrong expected value in my own test; I corrected the test, not the implementation.",
